[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the web service on srv1 (the internal HTTP/HTTPS server that hosts campus sites). DNS is healthy.

Mechanism: All “campus” hostnames resolve via internal DNS to 10.5.0.4 (srv1). Browsers then attempt to connect to 10.5.0.4 on TCP/80 (and/or 443). Because no web server is listening, the TCP connection fails immediately. Many browsers report this as “site can’t be found,” which can be mistaken for a DNS failure even though name resolution succeeds.

Localization: srv1, interface eth0 (10.5.0.4). DNS (named) is up and authoritative for jeremysitlab.com (wildcard A -> 10.5.0.4). HTTP/HTTPS daemons are not running and no sockets are listening on 80/443.

Evidence:
- From all office PCs, DNS resolves: getent/dig return 10.5.0.4 for srv1.jeremysitlab.com and other campus names.
- Connectivity to srv1 is fine (ICMP ping OK from pc1/pc2/pc3).
- On srv1, netstat shows only DNS ports (53, 953) listening; no tcp/80 or 443.
- systemctl shows apache2 loaded but inactive (dead); no nginx/httpd processes present.
- BIND is running (named -c /etc/bind/named.conf) and db.jeremysitlab has wildcard A to 10.5.0.4.

Root cause: The HTTP service on srv1 is stopped/not running, leaving no web server to answer requests.

Corrective fix: Start and enable the web server on srv1 (e.g., systemctl start apache2; systemctl enable apache2), or deploy nginx/httpd to listen on 10.5.0.4:80/443. Verify it serves the expected virtual hosts and add a health check so service restarts automatically after failures.
=== END ===